  "checklistTitle": "Terrace care checklist",
  "checklistSubtitle": "Weight, drainage, waterproofing, monsoon and heat",
  "checklistIntro": "Practical points for terraces and balconies. This is general guidance, not a structural assessment of your building.",
  "checkLoadTitle": "Check the roof can take the weight",
  "checkLoadBody": "Under the Indian loading code, a flat roof you can walk on is designed for about 150 kg per square metre of people and objects. Wet soil, pots and stored water add up quickly. Ask a structural engineer before adding raised beds, large planters or water tanks.",
  "checkLoadSources": "Sources: IS 875 (Part 2), Bureau of Indian Standards; ICAR Indian Horticulture",
  "checkWallsTitle": "Keep heavy containers over walls",
  "checkWallsBody": "Place grow bags and heavy pots where there is a wall underneath, not in the middle of an open span.",
  "checkWallsSources": "Source: Kerala Agricultural University",
  "checkDrainTitle": "Keep water draining",
  "checkDrainBody": "Every container needs drainage holes. Raise pots on bricks or pot feet so water runs away, empty saucers after watering, and keep the roof outlets clear.",
  "checkDrainSources": "Sources: University of Maryland Extension; Kerala Agricultural University",
  "checkWaterproofTitle": "Protect the waterproofing",
  "checkWaterproofBody": "Make sure the roof is waterproofed with a root- and rot-resistant layer before planting. After each crop, shift bags and bricks slightly so no patch of roof stays permanently wet.",
  "checkWaterproofSources": "Sources: ICAR Indian Horticulture; Kerala Agricultural University",
  "checkMonsoonTitle": "Prepare for the monsoon",
  "checkMonsoonBody": "Overwatering is the most common cause of container plant loss. In very wet spells, move pots under cover, remove saucers, and check the soil before watering even after rain.",
  "checkMonsoonSources": "Sources: Royal Horticultural Society",
  "checkHeatTitle": "Protect plants from summer heat",
  "checkHeatBody": "Small, dark and metal pots heat up and dry out fast, and south- and west-facing spots dry quickest. Group pots together, mulch the soil, and water early in the morning.",
  "checkHeatSources": "Sources: Royal Horticultural Society; University of Maryland Extension",

